\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces Validation sweeps used to choose the ridge penalty and the KNN neighbour count. Top row: ridge $\alpha $ on a log scale for raw OFF (solid) and raw ON (dashed) input, with validation $R^2$ (left) and RMSE (right); the dotted line marks the selected $\alpha =10^5$. Bottom row: KNN neighbours for the OFF bank, ON bank, and ON calibration. ``Off bank'' is the 17\,568 stimulation-off training windows used as the nearest-neighbour memory, and ``ON calibration'' is the $k$ labelled stimulated trials of a draw; see the text for the difference. Selection uses mean validation $R^2$; RMSE is shown for comparison. The current code tunes on ON validation sessions, and the OFF curves show the no-stimulation split for reference. PARRM overlaps the raw ridge curves.}}{12}{figure.9}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:sweeps}{{9}{12}{Validation sweeps used to choose the ridge penalty and the KNN neighbour count. Top row: ridge $\alpha $ on a log scale for raw OFF (solid) and raw ON (dashed) input, with validation $R^2$ (left) and RMSE (right); the dotted line marks the selected $\alpha =10^5$. Bottom row: KNN neighbours for the OFF bank, ON bank, and ON calibration. ``Off bank'' is the 17\,568 stimulation-off training windows used as the nearest-neighbour memory, and ``ON calibration'' is the $k$ labelled stimulated trials of a draw; see the text for the difference. Selection uses mean validation $R^2$; RMSE is shown for comparison. The current code tunes on ON validation sessions, and the OFF curves show the no-stimulation split for reference. PARRM overlaps the raw ridge curves}{figure.9}{}}
